= Database — Technical =

== Relational model ==
A relation is a set of tuples over attributes.

Relational algebra supplies operators such as selection, projection, join, union and difference.

SQL implements a practical, richer query language with bags, nulls and extensions.

== Transactions ==
ACID:

<pre>
atomicity
consistency
isolation
durability
</pre>

describes transaction goals, but “consistency” here means preservation of application/database invariants, not [[Consistency (logic)|logical consistency]] in general.

== Concurrency control ==
Serializable schedules reproduce some serial execution.

Systems use locking, timestamp ordering, MVCC, optimistic control, or hybrid approaches.

== Storage and indexes ==
B-trees, LSM trees, hashing, columnar formats and buffer management optimize different access/update patterns.

== Distributed data ==
Replication and partitioning introduce distributed transaction, consensus and consistency-model questions.

